WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.131 --> 00:00:02.759
(inspiring music)

00:00:02.759 --> 00:00:06.877
- I'm Danny Chew, and I’m the million-mile man.

00:00:06.877 --> 00:00:10.589
I wanna ride bicycles a
million miles over my lifetime.

00:00:10.589 --> 00:00:13.339
(gears grinding)

00:00:17.537 --> 00:00:20.833
I set the million-mile goal, uh,
sometime when I was in college.

00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:22.732
I just fell in love with cycling,

00:00:22.732 --> 00:00:25.388
and about the same time, I
created a ride in Pittsburgh

00:00:25.388 --> 00:00:27.242
called the Dirty Dozen.

00:00:27.242 --> 00:00:30.508
I found out that the longer the
race was, the better I did,

00:00:30.508 --> 00:00:35.256
and I won the Race Across
America in 1996 and 1999.

00:00:35.256 --> 00:00:38.243
(machine whirring)

00:00:38.243 --> 00:00:41.378
So, I was, uh, doin' one of my trips that I do

00:00:41.378 --> 00:00:44.067
where I try to do 1,000 miles in one week.

00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:46.872
I just got dizzy or something
and veered off to the left

00:00:46.872 --> 00:00:48.920
and I'm in the ditch before I know it,

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:52.291
and I can't move anything
below my chest, I can't get up.

00:00:52.291 --> 00:00:55.790
To suddenly have that freedom
where I can go anywhere

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:58.945
my legs will take me and have
it taken away in a second,

00:00:58.945 --> 00:01:01.528
that was, it was tough to take.

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:03.992
I mean, when I was in that hospital,

00:01:03.992 --> 00:01:07.429
I was very depressed those
few weeks after the accident.

00:01:07.429 --> 00:01:08.840
(groaning)

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.931
So, I'm basically about
seven and a 1/2 months out

00:01:10.931 --> 00:01:15.014
from the accident now, and my
condition is amazingly better

00:01:15.014 --> 00:01:17.982
than, obviously, compared to
the day after the accident.

00:01:17.982 --> 00:01:20.862
I expected to take at least a
year to get my arms big enough

00:01:20.862 --> 00:01:24.222
to be able to do a decent
speed on the hand cycle.

00:01:24.222 --> 00:01:27.123
I still need about 210,000 miles,

00:01:27.123 --> 00:01:29.096
but if I can get up to where I can average

00:01:29.096 --> 00:01:30.739
200 miles a week on a hand cycle,

00:01:30.739 --> 00:01:33.740
I could still get a million
miles 21 years from now.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:37.260
I'd be 75 then, and I hope
to live to be at least that.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.253
- That's great, I'm very
proud of you though.

00:01:39.253 --> 00:01:41.457
You've come a long way.

00:01:41.457 --> 00:01:43.205
- [Danny] When you have a
horrible spinal cord injury

00:01:43.205 --> 00:01:45.276
like that, you two choices,
you can just sort of

00:01:45.276 --> 00:01:48.913
curl up and you know, be
miserable, or think of it

00:01:48.913 --> 00:01:52.369
as a new challenge to tackle,
which is what I've done.

00:01:52.369 --> 00:01:54.821
As a paralyzed person,
you're stuck in a wheelchair

00:01:54.821 --> 00:01:56.892
most of the time that
you're awake, but at least

00:01:56.892 --> 00:01:59.504
when I'm on the hand cycle,
you're not in a wheel chair.

00:01:59.504 --> 00:02:02.214
You're on a machine like a bicycle.

00:02:02.214 --> 00:02:03.814
So, there's a sense of freedom there

00:02:03.814 --> 00:02:07.932
that you get away from the
wheelchair, and it's really nice.

00:02:07.932 --> 00:02:10.652
The million-mile goal will
take me a little bit longer

00:02:10.652 --> 00:02:13.926
than I originally planned,
but I feel like I owe it

00:02:13.926 --> 00:02:16.412
to my fans and the people
that support me that I have to

00:02:16.412 --> 00:02:20.579
keep going and, you know,
chasing this million-mile dream.

00:02:23.948 --> 00:02:26.948
(cheerful jingling)

